\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 7. Model taxonomy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Two independent axes. Confusing them is the most common mistake.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Axis 1: free vs paid\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Free-ish providers: OpenRouter, Groq (spelled with a Q, not Grok), Gemini free tier.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Paid: OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, and the rest.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Nothing is truly free. If a model is free, your data is the payment.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Axis 2: closed source vs open source\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Closed source:
\f1\b0  weights never released. You cannot host or fine-tune Claude Opus, Haiku, or GPT-5.5. Coca-Cola recipe.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Open source:
\f1\b0  weights published, so you can self-host on your own infra via Ollama or LM Studio. DeepSeek is the class example, with 1.5B / 14B / 32B / larger variants. Bigger means more RAM/VRAM and higher infra cost; smaller means cheaper, slower, and less capable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Key statement from class:
\f1\b0  OpenRouter gives you closed-source models for free or cheap, but you still cannot run them on your machine, because you don't have the weights.\
Llama licensing (Q&A): royalty-free commercial license, no per-token fee. The restriction kicks in above 700 million monthly active users.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 8. OpenRouter crash course\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 One key, 100+ models, multiple upstream providers per model.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The models page shows uptime, throughput, latency, and per-million-token in/out price for each provider serving a model. Calling OpenAI directly has lower latency than routing through Azure via OpenRouter.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
For common models, OpenRouter charges the same as going direct. First calls may work off roughly $1 of complimentary credit, which is not the same as free.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Free models
\f1\b0  exist (example used: NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Super). Limits: 20 requests per minute and 50 requests per day. Spending $10 in credits raises the daily limit to about 1000. Free calls are deprioritized and less reliable.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f5\b\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
openrouter/auto
\f1\b0\fs24  picks a model for you. There is also a 
\f0\b free models router
\f1\b0  that auto-routes across free models only.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 model = init_chat_model("openrouter:openrouter/auto", api_key=OPENROUTER_KEY)\
model = init_chat_model("openrouter:nvidia/nemotron-3-super:free", ...)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 Install 
\f2\fs26 langchain-openrouter
\f1\fs24  first.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Activity and Logs pages show each call: model used, tokens in/out, cost, which key, and which app made the call. OpenRouter can see that LangChain is the caller.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 When to use OpenRouter:
\f1\b0  heavy experimentation, testing many models, avoiding ten separate accounts and keys. Mayank uses it on a client project for exactly this reason.\

\f0\b When not to:
\f1\b0  production paths where you know the model. Go direct for lower latency. Never build logic that depends on auto-routing, because the selection logic is OpenRouter's USP, is undisclosed, can pick a cheaper model, and can change. Their incentive is their own cost and latency, not your quality.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
