\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 9. Q&A highlights\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Data privacy.
\f1\b0  Free models generally train on your data. NVIDIA's free endpoint warns against uploading confidential data. Paid and enterprise contracts (OpenAI, Anthropic, Azure, Bedrock, GCP) usually exclude training. Read the terms; they change. Inside a company, use only what the company approves. For PII, use guardrails; OpenRouter itself is not the control point.\

\f0\b Bedrock vs calling Anthropic directly.
\f1\b0  Performance is roughly the same. Bedrock wins on existing AWS contracts, compliance, and guardrails your company already runs. Direct calls give slightly lower latency. Mayank prefers Bedrock; finds Vertex AI pricing hard to reason about and expensive.\

\f0\b Model routing inside your own app.
\f1\b0  No framework gives you the logic. The approach discussed (rule-based filter, then a classifier, then an LLM as last resort) is sound. Sending every query to an LLM just to choose a model doubles your token spend.\

\f0\b Prompt caching.
\f1\b0  The provider caches KV values from the transformer for repeated prefixes, so you don't resend and reprocess the same tokens. Cache the system message, early context-setting messages, and uploaded reference material. A context composer that changes per query defeats caching. Prompt ordering matters.\

\f0\b Middleware
\f1\b0  (preview of a later class): everything that happens before and after the model call. Logging and analytics, prompt transformation, tool selection, output formatting, rate limiting, guardrails. Example: stopping a user's Aadhaar number before it reaches the model, or stopping the bot from abusing a user and ending up in a screenshot.\

